  "body": {
    "id": "thm:lipschitz-calibration",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "On the full declared class with \\(d=1\\), \\(\\alpha=\\beta=1\\), and \\(\\kappa=2\\), there exist \\(c_0,C_0>0\\), depending only on \\(L\\), such that jointly for every \\(n\\geq2\\) and \\(0\\leq\\epsilon\\leq1/10\\), \\[c_0\\{n^{-2/5}+\\epsilon^{1/2}\\}\\leq\\mathfrak R_n(\\epsilon;1,1,1,2,L)\\leq\\sup_{(P,Q,R)\\in\\mathcal C_{1,1,1,2,L}(\\epsilon)}\\mathbb E_{R^n}|T_{\\mathrm{Lip}}-\\theta(P)|\\leq C_0\\{n^{-2/5}+\\epsilon^{1/2}\\}.\\] When \\(b_n\\leq1/4\\), the sharper pre-optimization bound is \\[\\sup_{(P,Q,R)\\in\\mathcal C_{1,1,1,2,L}(\\epsilon)}\\mathbb E_{R^n}|T_{\\mathrm{Lip}}-\\theta(P)|\\leq C_0\\left[h_n^2+(nh_n)^{-1/2}+\\epsilon h_n^{-2}+\\exp\\{-c_0nh_n^{7/2}\\}\\right].\\] For each body cell \\(D\\subseteq[r_j,2r_j]\\), writing \\(q_D=Q\\{X\\in D\\}\\), its weighted contamination shift is at most \\(C_0\\epsilon q_D/r_j^2\\), and \\(\\sum_Dq_D\\leq1\\); the reused boundary slab \\(B_n\\) contributes at most \\(C_0\\epsilon q_{B_n}/h_n^2\\). These bounds depend only on total contaminating mass and therefore cover both point masses and diffuse within-cell contamination. The estimator uses \\(O(n\\log(1/h_n)+h_n^{-1})\\) arithmetic operations and \\(O(h_n^{-1})\\) memory.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "d",
      "alpha",
      "beta",
      "kappa",
      "L",
      "c_0",
